/*!
    \page tutorial-scenes.html
    \title Tutorial 4: Scenes and the Game Window
    \previouspage {Tutorial 3: The Felgo Project}
    \nextpage {Tutorial 5: A Game Core Like an MMORPG Server}

    Felgo splits a game into one \c GameWindow and several \c Scene items. We have three
    scenes: menu, game and game over.

    \section1 GameWindow

    \quotefromfile qml/Main.qml
    \skipto GameWindow {
    \printuntil state: "menu"

    \c screenWidth and \c screenHeight only matter on desktop. On phones the window fills
    the screen.

    \section1 A scene with a fixed size

    Design every scene for one size. Felgo scales it to the screen:

    \quotefromfile qml/scenes/GameScene.qml
    \skipto width: 640
    \printuntil scaleMode

    With \c letterbox the aspect ratio stays the same and free space becomes bars. To fill
    the bars too, anchor backgrounds to \c gameWindowAnchorItem:

    \code
    Rectangle {
        anchors.fill: gameScene.gameWindowAnchorItem
        color: "#111814"
    }
    \endcode

    \section1 Switching scenes

    Hide every scene by default and show the right one with a state on the window:

    \quotefromfile qml/Main.qml
    \skipto states: [
    \printuntil ]

    Each scene has this, so the change fades in and out:

    \code
    opacity: 0
    visible: opacity > 0
    Behavior on opacity { NumberAnimation { duration: 250 } }
    \endcode

    Switching is one line:

    \code
    gameWindow.state = "game"
    \endcode

    \section1 The menu

    The menu shows one card per race and a Felgo \c AppButton. Each card shows the first
    frame of a sprite sheet:

    \code
    TexturePackerAnimatedSprite {
        source: "qrc:/assets/creatures/orc/orc.json"
        frameNames: ["south_0.png"]
    }
    \endcode
*/
